The current version passes:

13
00:01:03.189 --> 00:01:05.813
189 terms, 0 problems.

14
00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:10.466
Interview-specific terms are in Chs 57 to 61.

15
00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:13.818
For questions on a term, use the Full Question Index.

16
00:01:15.168 --> 00:01:16.021
Part 1.

17
00:01:16.301 --> 00:01:17.368
0 to 9.

18
00:01:18.618 --> 00:01:20.410
2PC (two-phase commit):

19
00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:34.813
Atomic commit protocol: a coordinator asks every participant to prepare, then tells all to commit or all to abort; blocks if the coordinator fails after prepare. then Ch 13 Distributed Transactions.

20
00:01:35.093 --> 00:01:36.757
3 to 2-1 rule:

21
00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:38.317
Backup rule of thumb:

22
00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:48.346
3 copies, on 2 different media, 1 off-site (or in another region or account). then Ch 27 Reliability and Disaster Recovery.

23
00:01:49.696 --> 00:01:50.571
Part 2.

24
00:01:50.851 --> 00:01:51.469
A.

25
00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:53.551
ACID:

26
00:01:53.831 --> 00:02:04.605
Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability, the guarantees of a database transaction. then Ch 05 Relational Databases Deep Dive.

27
00:02:04.885 --> 00:02:07.359
ADR (architecture decision record):

28
00:02:07.639 --> 00:02:18.797
A short document recording one decision: context, options, decision, consequences, and when to revisit it. then Ch 31 Architecture Decision Making.

29
00:02:19.077 --> 00:02:19.973
Aggregate:

30
00:02:20.253 --> 00:02:31.111
A cluster of domain objects changed together inside one transaction, with one root that enforces its invariants. then Ch 33 Domain-Driven Design.

31
00:02:32.461 --> 00:02:33.805
Anti-corruption layer:

32
00:02:34.085 --> 00:02:41.936
A translation layer that stops another context's model leaking into yours. then Ch 33 Domain-Driven Design.

33
00:02:42.216 --> 00:02:43.112
Anycast:

34
00:02:43.392 --> 00:02:53.632
One IP address announced from many locations; the network routes each client to the nearest. then Ch 08 Load Balancing, Proxies and Gateways.

35
00:02:53.912 --> 00:02:55.192
API gateway:

36
00:02:55.472 --> 00:03:05.605
Edge component that routes, authenticates, rate-limits and shapes requests for backend services. then Ch 08 Load Balancing, Proxies and Gateways.

37
00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.293
At-least-once delivery:

38
00:03:07.573 --> 00:03:16.107
Every message is delivered one or more times; consumers must tolerate duplicates. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

39
00:03:17.457 --> 00:03:18.843
At-most-once delivery:

40
00:03:19.123 --> 00:03:27.955
Every message is delivered zero or one time; losses are possible, duplicates are not. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

41
00:03:28.235 --> 00:03:29.302
Availability:

42
00:03:29.582 --> 00:03:38.457
The fraction of time (or requests) a system serves correctly, often expressed in nines. then Ch 01 What System Design Is.

43
00:03:38.737 --> 00:03:39.547
Avro:

44
00:03:39.827 --> 00:03:48.510
A binary serialization format with reader and writer schemas resolved at read time. then Ch 28 Schema and Data Evolution.

45
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:50.756
Part 3.

46
00:03:51.036 --> 00:03:51.719
B.

47
00:03:52.969 --> 00:03:54.718
Back-of-the-envelope estimate:

48
00:03:54.998 --> 00:04:05.281
A quick order-of-magnitude calculation of load, storage or bandwidth to guide design choices. then Ch 02 Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation.

49
00:04:05.561 --> 00:04:06.371
Backfill:

50
00:04:06.651 --> 00:04:16.635
Populating a new column or store with historical data, usually in batches alongside live writes. then Ch 28 Schema and Data Evolution.

51
00:04:16.915 --> 00:04:17.875
Backpressure:

52
00:04:18.155 --> 00:04:28.054
Signalling upstream to slow down when a component cannot keep up, instead of queueing without bound. then Ch 19 Concurrency at Scale in Java.

53
00:04:29.404 --> 00:04:30.599
Base62:

54
00:04:30.879 --> 00:04:39.007
Encoding numbers with 0 to 9, a, z and A, Z, used for short URL codes. then Ch 38 Case Study:

55
00:04:39.287 --> 00:04:41.825
URL Shortener and SMS Short Links.

56
00:04:42.105 --> 00:04:44.153
BFF (backend for frontend):

57
00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:53.116
A backend tailored to one client type (app, web) that aggregates and shapes calls for it. then Ch 34 Modern Architecture Styles.

58
00:04:53.396 --> 00:04:54.612
Blast radius:

59
00:04:54.892 --> 00:05:03.575
How many users or systems one failure or bad change can affect. then Ch 27 Reliability and Disaster Recovery.

60
00:05:03.855 --> 00:05:04.836
Bloom filter:

61
00:05:05.116 --> 00:05:14.503
A compact probabilistic set: definitely not present or probably present, with a tunable false-positive rate. then Ch 53 Case Study:

62
00:05:14.783 --> 00:05:17.193
Web Crawler (Competitor Price Monitoring).

63
00:05:18.543 --> 00:05:19.802
Blue-green deployment:

64
00:05:20.082 --> 00:05:29.362
Running two production environments and switching traffic from the old (blue) to the new (green) at once. then Ch 26 Deployment and Infrastructure.

65
00:05:29.642 --> 00:05:30.943
BM25:

66
00:05:31.223 --> 00:05:39.991
A standard relevance-scoring function based on term frequency, document frequency and document length. then Ch 22 Search Systems.

67
00:05:40.271 --> 00:05:41.487
Bounded context:

68
00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:51.090
A boundary within which a model and its language are consistent; the unit of ownership in DDD. then Ch 33 Domain-Driven Design.

69
00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:52.223
Bulkhead:

70
00:05:52.503 --> 00:06:02.103
Isolating resources (threads, connections, pools) per dependency so one failure cannot exhaust all. then Ch 18 Resilience Patterns.

71
00:06:03.453 --> 00:06:04.371
Part 4.

72
00:06:04.651 --> 00:06:05.333
C.

73
00:06:06.583 --> 00:06:07.735
Cache stampede:

74
00:06:08.015 --> 00:06:18.661
Many requests miss the same expired key at once and overload the source; prevented by locking, coalescing or early refresh. then Ch 07 Caching.

75
00:06:18.941 --> 00:06:19.986
Cache-aside:

76
00:06:20.266 --> 00:06:28.586
The application reads the cache, falls back to the database on a miss, and fills the cache itself. then Ch 07 Caching.

77
00:06:28.866 --> 00:06:30.509
Calibration (interviewers):

78
00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:38.853
Aligning interviewers' scoring through anchored rubrics and shared review so verdicts are consistent. then Ch 60 Mock Interviews.

79
00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:40.221
Canary release:

80
00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:50.122
Sending a small share of traffic to a new version and widening only if its metrics stay healthy. then Ch 26 Deployment and Infrastructure.

81
00:06:51.472 --> 00:06:52.688
CAP theorem:

82
00:06:52.968 --> 00:07:02.675
During a network partition a distributed store must choose between consistency and availability. then Ch 12 Consistency, Time and Consensus.

83
00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:05.067
CDC (change data capture):

84
00:07:05.347 --> 00:07:16.035
Streaming a database's committed changes (usually from its log) to other systems. then Ch 15 Event-Driven Architecture, CQRS and Event Sourcing.

85
00:07:16.315 --> 00:07:18.619
CDN (content delivery network):

86
00:07:18.899 --> 00:07:25.277
Edge caches close to users that serve static and cacheable content. then Ch 52 Case Study:

87
00:07:25.557 --> 00:07:27.691
Object Storage and Media Pipeline.

88
00:07:29.041 --> 00:07:30.598
Cell-based architecture:

89
00:07:30.878 --> 00:07:41.609
Splitting a system into independent full copies (cells), each serving a subset of users, to bound blast radius. then Ch 34 Modern Architecture Styles.

90
00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:43.126
Chaos engineering:

91
00:07:43.406 --> 00:07:52.985
Deliberately injecting failures in controlled experiments to verify resilience. then Ch 27 Reliability and Disaster Recovery.

92
00:07:53.265 --> 00:07:54.353
Choreography:

93
00:07:54.633 --> 00:08:02.206
Services react to each other's events without a central coordinator. then Ch 34 Modern Architecture Styles.

94
00:08:02.486 --> 00:08:03.553
Circuit breaker:

95
00:08:03.833 --> 00:08:13.454
Stops calling a failing dependency for a while after errors exceed a threshold, then probes before resuming. then Ch 18 Resilience Patterns.

96
00:08:14.804 --> 00:08:15.700
Clock skew:

97
00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:26.711
The difference between clocks on different machines; why wall-clock timestamps cannot order distributed events safely. then Ch 12 Consistency, Time and Consensus.

98
00:08:26.991 --> 00:08:28.036
Connection pool:

99
00:08:28.316 --> 00:08:38.193
A bounded set of reused database connections; its size is a key capacity limit. then Ch 05 Relational Databases Deep Dive.

100
00:08:38.473 --> 00:08:39.689
Consistent hashing:

101
00:08:39.969 --> 00:08:49.484
Mapping keys and nodes onto a ring so adding or removing a node moves only a small share of keys. then Ch 11 Partitioning and Sharding.

102
00:08:50.834 --> 00:08:51.773
Consumer group:

103
00:08:52.053 --> 00:09:00.821
Kafka consumers sharing a group id divide a topic's partitions among themselves. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

104
00:09:01.101 --> 00:09:02.103
Consumer lag:

105
00:09:02.383 --> 00:09:09.573
How far a consumer is behind the latest message in a partition. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

106
00:09:09.853 --> 00:09:11.047
Contract testing:

107
00:09:11.327 --> 00:09:22.463
Verifying that a provider still satisfies the requests and responses its consumers depend on (for example, Pact). then Ch 29 Testing Distributed Systems.

108
00:09:22.743 --> 00:09:24.151
Coordinated omission:

109
00:09:24.431 --> 00:09:33.775
A load-testing error where a stalled system also stalls request sending, hiding the real latency. then Ch 25 Performance Engineering.

110
00:09:35.125 --> 00:09:36.171
Cost per order:

111
00:09:36.451 --> 00:09:44.984
A unit-economics metric: infrastructure cost divided by orders served. then Ch 35 Cost Engineering and FinOps.

112
00:09:45.264 --> 00:09:46.352
Count-Min Sketch:

113
00:09:46.632 --> 00:09:55.187
A small probabilistic table that estimates item frequencies in a stream, never underestimating. then Ch 51 Case Study:

114
00:09:55.467 --> 00:09:58.091
Real-time Analytics and Top-K (Trending).

115
00:09:58.371 --> 00:09:59.437
CQRS:

116
00:09:59.717 --> 00:10:10.555
Command Query Responsibility Segregation: separate models for writes and for reads. then Ch 15 Event-Driven Architecture, CQRS and Event Sourcing.

117
00:10:11.905 --> 00:10:12.929
CRDT:

118
00:10:13.209 --> 00:10:21.486
A conflict-free replicated data type whose replicas can merge concurrent updates deterministically. then Ch 10 Replication.

119
00:10:21.766 --> 00:10:22.854
Crypto-shredding:

120
00:10:23.134 --> 00:10:33.929
Making data unreadable by deleting its encryption key, used for erasure in backups and logs. then Ch 36 Compliance, Governance and Data Residency.

121
00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:36.239
Part 5.

122
00:10:36.519 --> 00:10:37.244
D.

123
00:10:38.494 --> 00:10:39.881
DAG (workflow):

124
00:10:40.161 --> 00:10:47.649
A directed acyclic graph of jobs where each runs after its dependencies succeed. then Ch 50 Case Study:

125
00:10:47.929 --> 00:10:49.657
Distributed Job Scheduler.

126
00:10:49.937 --> 00:10:50.918
Dark store:

127
00:10:51.198 --> 00:10:57.875
A delivery-only warehouse serving quick-commerce orders from a small area. then Ch 46 Case Study:

128
00:10:58.155 --> 00:11:01.014
Grocery Checkout and Inventory Reservation.

129
00:11:01.294 --> 00:11:02.553
Data residency:

130
00:11:02.833 --> 00:11:13.542
A requirement that certain data be stored (and sometimes processed) within a given country or region. then Ch 36 Compliance, Governance and Data Residency.

131
00:11:13.822 --> 00:11:14.867
Dead-letter queue:

132
00:11:15.147 --> 00:11:24.043
A queue that holds messages that repeatedly fail processing, for inspection and replay. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

133
00:11:25.393 --> 00:11:27.356
Deterministic simulation testing:

134
00:11:27.636 --> 00:11:38.345
Running a whole distributed system in a simulated, seeded environment so failures can be reproduced exactly. then Ch 29 Testing Distributed Systems.

135
00:11:38.625 --> 00:11:39.927
Disagree and commit:

136
00:11:40.207 --> 00:11:49.188
Arguing a position with evidence before a decision, then fully supporting the decision once made. then Ch 61 Behavioural and Leadership.

137
00:11:49.468 --> 00:11:50.769
Distributed lock:

138
00:11:51.049 --> 00:12:00.436
A lock shared across processes; safe only with leases and fencing tokens. then Ch 20 Idempotency, IDs and Distributed Locks.

139
00:12:01.786 --> 00:12:03.215
Distributed monolith:

140
00:12:03.495 --> 00:12:13.394
Services that must be deployed or changed together, the costs of microservices without the independence. then Ch 34 Modern Architecture Styles.

141
00:12:13.674 --> 00:12:14.570
DLQ:

142
00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:18.967
See dead-letter queue. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

143
00:12:19.247 --> 00:12:20.143
DNS:

144
00:12:20.423 --> 00:12:28.914
The Domain Name System, mapping names to addresses through a hierarchy of cached lookups. then Ch 03 Networking Essentials.

145
00:12:29.194 --> 00:12:30.218
Domain event:

146
00:12:30.498 --> 00:12:38.306
A record that something meaningful happened in the domain, named in the past tense. then Ch 33 Domain-Driven Design.

147
00:12:39.656 --> 00:12:40.893
Double-entry ledger:

148
00:12:41.173 --> 00:12:49.408
Recording every money movement as equal debits and credits so balances always reconcile. then Ch 45 Case Study:

149
00:12:49.688 --> 00:12:50.733
Payment System.

150
00:12:51.013 --> 00:12:51.973
Durability:

151
00:12:52.253 --> 00:12:58.717
Once acknowledged, data survives crashes and failures. then Ch 01 What System Design Is.

152
00:13:00.067 --> 00:13:01.006
Part 6.

153
00:13:01.286 --> 00:13:01.926
E.

154
00:13:03.176 --> 00:13:04.328
Erasure coding:

155
00:13:04.608 --> 00:13:15.552
Splitting data into k data and m parity shards so any k of k+m can rebuild it, at lower overhead than replication. then Ch 52 Case Study:

156
00:13:15.832 --> 00:13:17.965
Object Storage and Media Pipeline.

157
00:13:18.245 --> 00:13:19.248
Error budget:

158
00:13:19.528 --> 00:13:30.344
The allowed unreliability under an SLO (for example, 0.1% of requests); spending it slows releases. then Ch 24 Observability.

159
00:13:30.624 --> 00:13:31.669
Event sourcing:

160
00:13:31.949 --> 00:13:43.427
Storing state as the sequence of events that produced it; current state is derived by replay. then Ch 15 Event-Driven Architecture, CQRS and Event Sourcing.

161
00:13:44.777 --> 00:13:45.822
Event storming:

162
00:13:46.102 --> 00:13:54.123
A workshop technique that maps a domain by laying out its events on a timeline. then Ch 33 Domain-Driven Design.

163
00:13:54.403 --> 00:13:55.939
Eventual consistency:

164
00:13:56.219 --> 00:14:05.115
Replicas converge to the same value if updates stop; reads may be stale meanwhile. then Ch 12 Consistency, Time and Consensus.

165
00:14:05.395 --> 00:14:06.953
Exactly-once processing:

166
00:14:07.233 --> 00:14:18.305
Each input affects the output once, achieved by combining at-least-once delivery with idempotency or transactions. then Ch 14 Messaging and Event Streaming.

167
00:14:18.585 --> 00:14:19.993
Expand or contract:

168
00:14:20.273 --> 00:14:30.001
Changing a schema in backward-compatible steps: add the new, migrate, switch, then remove the old. then Ch 28 Schema and Data Evolution.

169
00:14:31.351 --> 00:14:32.289
Part 7.

170
00:14:32.569 --> 00:14:33.209
F.

171
00:14:34.459 --> 00:14:35.377
Failover:

172
00:14:35.657 --> 00:14:45.406
Moving service from a failed component (for example, a primary database) to a standby. then Ch 27 Reliability and Disaster Recovery.

173
00:14:45.686 --> 00:14:46.539
Fan-out:

174
00:14:46.819 --> 00:14:58.915
One request or event multiplied into many downstream calls or writes, a feed request into feature lookups, or a post into every follower's feed. then Ch 43 Case Study:

175
00:14:59.195 --> 00:15:01.137
Home Feed and Recommendations.

176
00:15:01.417 --> 00:15:02.462
Feature flag:

177
00:15:02.742 --> 00:15:10.742
A runtime switch that turns code paths on or off without redeploying. then Ch 26 Deployment and Infrastructure.

178
00:15:12.092 --> 00:15:13.265
Fencing token:

179
00:15:13.545 --> 00:15:24.127
A monotonically increasing number checked by the resource, so a stale lock holder's writes are rejected. then Ch 20 Idempotency, IDs and Distributed Locks.

180
00:15:24.407 --> 00:15:25.516
Fitness function:

181
00:15:25.796 --> 00:15:35.908
An automated check that an architectural property (dependency rules, latency, cost) still holds. then Ch 31 Architecture Decision Making.

182
00:15:36.188 --> 00:15:37.148
Flip point:

183
00:15:37.428 --> 00:15:45.257
The weighting or value at which a decision matrix would choose a different option. then Ch 31 Architecture Decision Making.

184
00:15:46.607 --> 00:15:47.482
Part 8.

185
00:15:47.762 --> 00:15:48.509
G.

186
00:15:49.759 --> 00:15:50.676
Geohash:

187
00:15:50.956 --> 00:15:59.105
Encoding latitude and longitude into a string whose shared prefixes mean nearby cells. then Ch 47 Case Study:

188
00:15:59.385 --> 00:16:01.348
Delivery Dispatch and Live Tracking.

189
00:16:01.628 --> 00:16:03.036
Graceful degradation:

190
00:16:03.316 --> 00:16:11.785
Keeping the core function working with reduced features when dependencies fail. then Ch 18 Resilience Patterns. gRPC:

191
00:16:12.065 --> 00:16:20.961
A high-performance RPC framework using HTTP or 2 and Protocol Buffers. then Ch 04 API Design.

192
00:16:22.311 --> 00:16:23.207
Part 9.

193
00:16:23.487 --> 00:16:24.170
H.

194
00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:26.743
Head-of-line blocking:

195
00:16:27.023 --> 00:16:34.596
One slow item holding up everything behind it on a connection or queue. then Ch 03 Networking Essentials.

196
00:16:34.876 --> 00:16:35.751
Heartbeat:

197
00:16:36.031 --> 00:16:44.244
A periodic signal that a node is alive, used for failure detection. then Ch 12 Consistency, Time and Consensus.

198
00:16:44.524 --> 00:16:45.655
Hedged request:

199
00:16:45.935 --> 00:16:56.004
Sending a duplicate request after a delay and using whichever answer returns first, to cut tail latency. then Ch 25 Performance Engineering.

200
00:16:57.354 --> 00:16:58.954
Hexagonal architecture:

201
00:16:59.234 --> 00:17:08.621
Ports and adapters: domain logic at the centre, infrastructure plugged in through interfaces. then Ch 34 Modern Architecture Styles.

202
00:17:08.901 --> 00:17:09.925
Hinted handoff:

203
00:17:10.205 --> 00:17:18.439
A node temporarily stores writes for an unavailable replica and forwards them when it returns. then Ch 54 Case Study:

204
00:17:18.719 --> 00:17:20.554
Distributed Key-Value Store.

205
00:17:20.834 --> 00:17:22.562
Hot key or hot partition:

206
00:17:22.842 --> 00:17:29.562
A key or shard receiving a disproportionate share of traffic. then Ch 11 Partitioning and Sharding.

207
00:17:30.912 --> 00:17:32.149
HyperLogLog:

208
00:17:32.429 --> 00:17:40.152
A probabilistic structure that estimates the number of distinct items in a few kilobytes. then Ch 51 Case Study:

209
00:17:40.432 --> 00:17:43.056
Real-time Analytics and Top-K (Trending).

210
00:17:44.406 --> 00:17:45.259
Part 10.

211
00:17:45.539 --> 00:17:46.222
I.

212
00:17:47.472 --> 00:17:48.731
Idempotency key:

213
00:17:49.011 --> 00:17:59.528
A client-supplied key that lets the server recognise retries and return the original result. then Ch 20 Idempotency, IDs and Distributed Locks.

214
00:17:59.808 --> 00:18:00.832
Idempotent:

215
00:18:01.112 --> 00:18:09.901
An operation that has the same effect whether applied once or many times. then Ch 20 Idempotency, IDs and Distributed Locks.

216
00:18:10.181 --> 00:18:11.312
Interviewer card:

217
00:18:11.592 --> 00:18:20.019
A mock interviewer's sheet: prompt, hidden requirements, follow-up chains, curveball, level anchors. then Ch 60 Mock Interviews.

218
00:18:20.299 --> 00:18:21.152
Invariant:

219
00:18:21.432 --> 00:18:29.112
A rule that must always hold, for example, stock never goes below zero. then Ch 33 Domain-Driven Design.

220
00:18:30.462 --> 00:18:31.635
Inverted index:

221
00:18:31.915 --> 00:18:39.745
A map from each term to the documents containing it; the core structure of full-text search. then Ch 22 Search Systems.

222
00:18:40.025 --> 00:18:41.305
Isolation level:

223
00:18:41.585 --> 00:18:52.123
How much concurrent transactions can see of each other (read committed, repeatable read, serializable). then Ch 05 Relational Databases Deep Dive.

224
00:18:53.473 --> 00:18:54.476
Part 11.

225
00:18:54.756 --> 00:18:55.460
